Psychophysiological comfort during active urban mobility: Environment and activity modulated physiological and perceived responses across diverse urban contexts 
1 Introduction
Background and gap

-Active mobility is being promoted in cities... 
-To enhance active mobility, cities have invested in xxx... 
-However, the mobility comfort conditions that active commuters experience are still to be understood. This is particularly relevant today in the face of climate change, overheating... 
-Prior papers that have done experimental campaigns have studied comfort when walking or biking (you need to go through a few). 
-For example, a paper looked at how biking comfort is xxx... 
-For example, a paper looked at perceived thermal comfort while walking through xxx... 
-Probably there is no paper comparing walking and biking results, and going through different urban environments. Or are there? 
-And from these studies, NONE have combined physiology, psychology, environmental sensing, and different active modes in the same field protocol and urban environment. 
  -Such an experimental study is necessary to better understand how physiological and perceived comfort vary based on: i) active mobility (walking or cycling); ii) the urban context  --  for example, green versus very impervious urban areas, calm vs. noisy, pedestrianized vs. trafficked.
  -Such understanding can contribute to more strategic urban planning investment and to the future planning of cycling routes and pedestrian areas in cities, at a time when environmental stressors are only becoming more acute.

This research presents the results of an experimental campaign performed in xxxx and xxxx. [description of it]
The hypotheses on which the campaign was based were:
 H1: Participants will show better physiological and perceived comfort in green than in impervious areas.
 H2: Physiological and perceived responses will be concordant (i.e., they correlate in direction).
 H3: Psychophysiological comfort will be more compromised during cycling than during walking.
 H4: The presence of noise and traffic will amplify thermal discomfort.
In the following sections, the methodology [xx] is described, followed by [xxx].

 








2 Methodology
Data collection process
Within-subject field campaign in Brussels; each participant completed repeated exposure phases across urban, green and tram environments.
Surveys were administered before/after exposure phases; sensors were checked during breaks; researcher followed participant with environmental backpack.
Experiment plan and phases
Main contrasts: green vs impervious urban; bike vs walk vs seated tram.
Phases to explain in paper: Biking in Urban (BikeU), Walking in Urban (WalkU), Biking in Green (BikeG), Walking in Green (WalkG) and Tram; include trajectory map and timeline figure.
Sensors and questionnaires
urban Climate Monitor (uCM) backpack: air temperature, humidity, radiation, wind, sound level, Global Positioning System (GPS) and comfort indices that include Universal Thermal Climate Index (UTCI)/Physiological Equivalent Temperature (PET).
Empatica Embrace Plus: electrodermal activity (EDA) tonic/phasic, photoplethysmography (PPG) heart rate, heart rate variability (HRV) root mean square of successive differences (rMSSD) and wrist temperature; physiological stream averaged to 10-sec epochs.
Questionnaires: State-Trait Anxiety Inventory (STAI-6), Perceived Restorativeness Scale (PRS-11), comfort, pleasantness, thermal comfort/perception; these operationalise psychological well-being and thermal perception.
Data analysis and correction
Sensor streams synchronised, path-matched, quality-checked and aggregated by participant/phase; final valid sample after filtering. [N = 11]
Hypothesis testing strategy
H1 and mobility contrasts: paired comparisons with effect sizes for green vs urban and bike vs walk.
H2: repeated-measures phase analysis across BikeU, WalkU, BikeG, WalkG and Tram.
H3-H4: Spearman correlations for physio-psychology alignment and environmental drivers such as temperature/noise.
3 Results
Hypothesis-level findings
H1 partly supported: green improved psychological outcomes, but not physiological outcomes. [Comfort d=-0.92, p=0.0119; pleasantness d=-1.45, p<0.001; PRS d=-2.58, p<0.001; STAI d=0.91, p=0.0127; EDA/HR/HRV p>0.22]
H2 partly supported only for psychological phase progression; physiological phase/acclimatisation effects were not significant. [Comfort eta2p=0.383, p<0.001; pleasantness eta2p=0.384, p<0.001; PRS eta2p=0.703, p<0.001; HR p=0.6003; HRV p=0.3637]
H3 weakly supported: physiology and psychology did not align consistently in the valid sample. [Most correlations non-significant at N=10-11]
H4 supported for environmental stressors: heat and noise were linked to poorer comfort. [Air temperature vs thermal comfort rho=-0.64, p=0.0170; sound level vs comfort rho=-0.55, p=0.0282]
H5 supported: mobility type affected physiological load and subjective comfort. [Bike lower HR d=-0.87, p=0.0164; lower EDA d=-0.77, p=0.0287; higher comfort d=0.87, p=0.0162; higher pleasantness d=1.02, p=0.0068]
